# The Recommended rule of the same name holds the phrases (`as needed`,
# `where applicable`). These words sit here, because human-written docs
# use them about three times as often as the phrases, and a word rule
# cannot see a criterion named a few words later (`an appropriate label
# matching topologyKey`). Each word counts only without `for` or `to`
# after it, the two words that most often introduce the criterion
# (`suitable for bootstrapping`). The fix names the criterion (`choose
# an appropriate timeout` becomes `choose a timeout longer than the
# slowest request`).
#
# `proper` is left out, because it names a math term (`a proper
# subset`), and `relevant` is left out, because it usually points at a
# named thing (`the relevant API reference`). `reasonably` counts only
# before an adjective or an adverb (`reasonably fast`), because before a
# verb it means plausibly (`you might reasonably expect`). Only
# `appropriate` passes after `as`, `where`, `when`, or `if`, because the
# Recommended rule reports those phrases (`where appropriate`), and one
# word should draw one finding. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter
# consumes the word before and narrows the finding back to
# `appropriate`.
#
# It hit 224 lines in the measure.py corpus, and about 15 of 25 sampled
# hits left the criterion unstated.
expr word [appropriately, properly, suitable, suitably, reasonable, sensible, sane]
expr fit [appropriate]
expr main [<(![as, where, when, if] @fit!(( )[for, to])), @fit>, (@word!(( )[for, to])), (reasonably [ADJ, ADV])]
let message "Vague criterion. Name the condition that makes it right, or drop the word."
let description "Flags `appropriate`, `properly`, `reasonable`, and similar words that leave the reader to supply a criterion."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The image is suitable for bootstrapping the control plane."
allows "The limit is appropriate to a single tenant."
allows "Choose a timeout longer than the slowest request."
allows "Restructure the manuals where appropriate."
allows "You might reasonably expect the cache to be warm."
